    "turn_text": "city chiefs this weekend to speak to law enforcement professionals from all over the country. Our good friend Commissioner Pat Ryder will be speaking, Tom Holman will be speaking, and I'm going to be sharing the stage talking about consent decrees and the fact that There is so much money that should be headed. towards the training, the education, towards resources and equipment for law enforcement agencies, but they are being held by these consent decrees. And quite frankly, many of these communities want the consent decrees to stay in place because under the Biden administration, we saw tens of millions of dollars going into people that are supposed to be the federal monitors. And really, what we've uncovered and what we've read about is the fact that in places like Seattle, You had the federal monitors and the leadership from the police department and in everyday conversation. I mean, this was an oversight. They were actually working together to just continue this to go on. Why? Because these oversight individuals and the federal monitors and the deputy monitors and all of these individuals were getting paid big money. And then you have these contracts going to what we're seeing doge cut now, these DEI contracts, these contracts to do studies into the most bizarre issues in these police departments. And I think that it really needs to come to an end. We need our Justice Department to step in to look at the consent decrees and to say, all right, listen, these departments have the ability to move forward and to do what's right by the community. And we've said it before, no cop goes out on the street and intends to have bad things happen. And there's nobody that dislikes bad cops more than good cops. So the good cops, the good law enforcement professionals want to go out there and do the right thing and keep their community safe. But they're being handcuffed because of these consent decrees. And instead of being able to spend money on technology, instead of being able to purchase equipment, instead of being able to invest in training, All this money is going to meet these consent decrees and to pay consultants and to pay these federal monitors. And it's something that I think, you know, we've seen places like Seattle and Baltimore, people are getting set up. And I think that the time has come where, you know, with everything going on with Doge and the fact that, you know, you have somebody like Cash Patel who wants to let cops be cops. This is the opportunity for it. And I hope that this administration takes that into consideration. that is great work and the new one of those cops you were a uh... cops cop you're great cop and uh... i would hope that there's something in your very near future that has you working with law enforcement again the final sixty",
